{"maxId":370,"entries":{"chapter-68-study-pain-management|Chapter 68 Study: Pain Management":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Acute pain: sudden onset, tied to a noxious stimulus, short duration of tissue healing (approximately ≤3 months) (p1055)":["u3"],"copy|Chronic (persistent) pain: persists or recurs for >3 months (p1055)":["u4"],"copy|High-impact chronic pain: limits life/work activities most or every day for the past 6 months; 8% of US adults (20 million) had it in 2016 (p1055)":["u5"],"copy|IASP/ICD-11 (2019) chronic pain classes: \"chronic primary pain\" (disease in its own right, eg fibromyalgia, nonspecific low back pain) vs six \"chronic secondary\" categories (musculoskeletal, neuropathic, headache/orofacial, visceral, cancer-related, postsurgical/posttraumatic) (p1055)":["u6"],"copy|Nociceptive pain (tissue injury/inflammation/mechanical) usually responds well to analgesics; neuropathic pain (lesion/disease of the somatosensory system) affects 7% to 10% of the population and is often persistent and difficult to treat in older adults (p1056–1057)":["u7"],"copy|Chronic pain prevalence in older adults: 25% to 75% (higher in residential care); 60% to 70% report multisite pain, >60% report multiple pain types (p1055)":["u8"],"copy|High-impact chronic pain prevalence by age band (2016): 11% (65–84 yr) vs 16% (≥85 yr) (p1055–1056)":["u9"],"copy|Peripheral sensitization → hyperalgesia and spread of pain; central sensitization → allodynia (nonpainful stimuli now activate pain pathways) (p1056)":["u10"],"copy|Age-related pain perception: ↓ sensitivity to low-intensity and heat pain; no strong age effect on pain tolerance — changes are subtle and of uncertain clinical relevance (p1057)":["u11"],"copy|Biopsychosocial model (Fig 68-1): bio (age, sex, comorbidity, fatigue, sleep, health behaviors) + psych (depression, anxiety, catastrophizing, fear avoidance) + social (race/ethnicity, SES, ageism, isolation) → outcomes (physical/cognitive function, QOL) (p1057–1058)":["u12"],"copy|Self-report is the gold standard for pain measurement (p1058)":["u13"],"copy|Unidimensional scales (single factor, eg intensity) vs multidimensional scales (intensity + interference + treatment history) — Tables 68-3/68-4 (p1059)":["u14"],"copy|In cognitive impairment/dementia: keep questions simple (\"Does this hurt?\"), use nonverbal observational scales, and involve caregivers (p1058)":["u15"],"copy|↓ gastric secretion/motility → altered absorption; ↑ fat, ↓ lean mass/total body water/albumin → altered distribution (more unbound drug with NSAIDs, TCAs); ↓ hepatic volume/blood flow and ↓ renal blood flow/GFR → ↓ clearance; ↓ lung elasticity/↑ chest wall rigidity → ↑ risk of respiratory depression with opioids (Table 68-5, p1059–1060)":["u16"],"copy|1 in 3 Medicare Part D beneficiaries received an opioid prescription (2016 analysis) (p1060)":["u17"],"copy|7.7% increase in opioid-overdose deaths in adults >65 yr, 2013→2014 (p1060)":["u18"],"copy|2016 CDC guideline for older adults: prefer immediate-release (rec #4), lowest effective dose (rec #5), follow-up 1 to 4 weeks after starting then at least every 3 months (rec #7); use state PDMPs (p1060)":["u19"],"copy|Start opioids at 25% to 50% of the younger-adult dose in older adults (p1060)":["u20"],"copy|Before increasing to 50 MME/day or more, pause to reassess benefits and risks and consider precautions. The 2022 CDC guideline for outpatient pain treatment uses dosage as a guidepost and rejects an inflexible 90-MME ceiling. It excludes cancer-related pain treatment, palliative care and end-of-life care (p1060 discusses the 2016 guideline).":["u21"],"copy|Opioid-induced hyperalgesia: pain worsens with increasing opioid dose (vs tolerance, where it doesn't) — manage by tapering and adding nonopioid/NMDA-antagonist therapy (p1061)":["u22"],"copy|Perioperative: continuous end-tidal CO2 monitoring for high-risk patients, especially the first 24 hours after surgery (p1062)":["u23"],"copy|Acetaminophen: 500–1000 mg q6h; max 4000 mg/day; max 2000–3000 mg/day with hepatic impairment or alcohol use (p1062)":["u24"],"copy|Table 68-7 singles out indomethacin and ketorolac as Beers 2019 \"avoid\" outright (high risk of GI toxicity and acute kidney injury) — a stronger caution than the general \"avoid long-term use\" that applies to ibuprofen, naproxen, meloxicam, diclofenac, and nabumetone (Table 68-7, p1063)":["u26"],"copy|SNRIs/TCAs modulate descending pain pathways; Beers 2019 flags both SNRIs and TCAs (amitriptyline, nortriptyline) as \"avoid\" with a history of falls/fractures, and TCAs additionally for anticholinergic burden (Table 68-8, p1063)":["u27"],"copy|Carbamazepine and oxcarbazepine are sodium-channel blockers used for selected neuropathic pain syndromes. HLA-B*15:02 increases the risk of SJS/TEN with either drug and is more frequent in several East, Southeast and South Asian populations. HLA-A*31:01 is associated with a broader range of carbamazepine reactions in several ancestries. Carbamazepine induces CYP enzymes and can reduce warfarin exposure. Combined lithium and carbamazepine can cause neurotoxicity (p1065, CPIC).":["u28"],"copy|Table 68-8: gabapentinoids (gabapentin, pregabalin) are contraindicated in renal impairment and should be avoided with concurrent opioid use; carbamazepine/oxcarbazepine carry a hyponatremia risk — monitor Na+ and drug levels (Table 68-8, p1063–1064)Current practice (not the book): renal impairment is not a labelled contraindication for gabapentin or pregabalin; both need renal dose reduction (incl. CrCl <15 and post-haemodialysis supplemental doses) — FDA/DailyMed gabapentin and Pfizer Lyrica labels.":["u29"],"copy|Benzodiazepines (eg clonazepam) for neuropathic pain: daily use >30 days risks physiologic dependence — taper, don't stop abruptly; tracked as lorazepam milligram equivalents (LMEs) in PDMPs (p1065)":["u30"],"copy|Lumbar epidural steroid injection (ESI): short-term relief (weeks to months); contraindications include coagulopathy, current anticoagulation, infection, uncontrolled diabetes, and immunosuppression (p1065)":["u31"],"copy|Facet-mediated and sacroiliac joint pain: diagnostic medial-branch/lateral-branch nerve blocks; 80% pain reduction/relief after the diagnostic block → proceed to radiofrequency denervation for longer relief (p1066)":["u32"],"copy|Vertebral compression fractures (VCF): 25% of postmenopausal women in the US affected; 40–50% of adults >80 yr have had one; 5× risk of a second VCF after the first; only ~25% result from falls (most from routine bending/lifting); most common at T12–L3; vertebroplasty/kyphoplasty contraindicated if vertebral height loss >66% (p1066)":["u33"],"copy|Book claim to flag: the chapter states knee OA prevalence is \"two times higher in men than women\" (p1067) — worth double-checking against other sources before quoting, since knee OA is more commonly described as female-predominant elsewhere":["u35"],"copy|Nonpharmacologic: physical therapy (including low-impact aquatic-based therapy/exercise for patients with chronic pain and stiffness), CBT/ACT/biofeedback, mindfulness-based stress reduction, and complementary therapies (acupuncture, massage) all have a role, especially in patients who can't tolerate medications or procedures (p1068)":["u36"],"recall|Recall":["u37"],"copy|row:DRUG|TH|DRUG":["u39"],"copy|row:DRUG|TH|INITIAL DOSING":["u40"],"copy|row:DRUG|TH|COMMENTS":["u41"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Oxycodone, immediate release (Roxicodone)":["u42"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|5–10 mg every 4–6 hours":["u43"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Titrate to comfort: continuous use for constant pain and intermittent use for episodic pain":["u44"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Fentanyl, transdermal (Duragesic)":["u45"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|25-mcg patch (every-72-hour dosing)":["u46"],"copy|row:undefined|TD|Should be avoided in opioid-naive patientsDose dependent on previous 24-hour total morphine milligram equivalent dose":["u47"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 68-6, p.1061":["u48","u81"],"copy|CHAPTER 68s / HAZZARD":["u49"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 68 — Pain Management; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u50"],"copy|Definitions & classification":["u51"],"copy|Epidemiology & pathophysiology":["u52"],"copy|Assessment":["u53"],"copy|Pharmacokinetic aging changes relevant to dosing (Table 68-5, p1060)":["u54"],"copy|Opioids":["u55"],"copy|Nonopioid & adjuvant medications":["u56"],"copy|Interventional & nonpharmacologic treatment":["u57"],"copy|2.":["u58"],"copy|What defines \"high-impact\" chronic pain, and how common was it in the US in 2016?":["u59"],"copy|Chronic pain that limits life or work activities on most or every day for the past 6 months; an estimated 8% of US adults (20 million people) had it in 2016 (p1055).":["u60"],"copy|4.":["u61"],"copy|Distinguish peripheral sensitization from central sensitization in chronic pain pathophysiology.":["u62"],"copy|Peripheral sensitization (inflammatory mediators like histamine/bradykinin lowering nociceptor threshold) causes hyperalgesia and spread of pain; central sensitization (maladaptive CNS plasticity) increases synaptic strength of pain pathways and can produce allodynia, where nonpainful stimuli trigger pain (p1056).":["u63"],"copy|6.":["u64"],"copy|What starting-dose adjustment does the chapter recommend for opioids in older adults, and what are the CDC's MME/day caution thresholds?":["u65"],"copy|The chapter suggests lower initial opioid doses in older adults, often 25–50% of a younger adult's dose (p1060).":["u66"],"copy|Choose the drug and dose for renal function, frailty and prior exposure.":["u66"],"copy|CDC 2022 recommends reassessment before reaching 50 MME/day and individualized decisions rather than a fixed 90-MME ceiling.":["u66"],"copy|8.":["u67"],"copy|What is the maximum total daily dose of acetaminophen, and what is the reduced maximum in hepatic impairment or a history of alcohol use?":["u68"],"copy|4000 mg/day maximum in general; 2000 to 3000 mg/day maximum with hepatic impairment or a history of alcohol use (p1062).":["u69"],"copy|9.":["u70"],"copy|Which two HLA alleles should prompt genotype screening before starting carbamazepine, and why?":["u71"],"copy|HLA-B15:02 is linked to carbamazepine- and oxcarbazepine-associated SJS/TEN.":["u72"],"copy|HLA-A31:01 is associated with several carbamazepine hypersensitivity reactions and occurs across multiple ancestries.":["u72"],"copy|The genes do not have the same geographic distribution (p1065, CPIC).":["u72"],"copy|10.":["u73"],"copy|After how many days of daily benzodiazepine use can older adults develop physiologic dependence, and how should the drug be stopped?":["u74"],"copy|More than 30 days of daily use; the drug should be gradually tapered rather than stopped abruptly, to avoid withdrawal (p1065).":["u75"],"copy|12.":["u76"],"copy|What proportion of adults over 80 have sustained a vertebral compression fracture (VCF), and what proportion of VCFs actually result from a fall?":["u77"],"copy|An estimated 40% to 50% of people older than 80 have had a VCF; only about a quarter of VCFs result from falls — most are precipitated by routine activities like bending or lifting (p1066).":["u78"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, p.1061 · Read source":["u79"],"copy|Answer":["u82","u83","u84","u85","u86","u87","u88"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u89"],"copy|Choosing a pain measure when the patient can select a color, and when severe dementia prevents self-rating — June 2023 Q23 and June 2026 Q91. PAINAD itself is on the other cited page.":["u90"],"copy|Telling ischemic, inflammatory and neuropathic pain apart — June 2026 Q26.":["u91"],"copy|Selecting an immediate-release opioid for metastatic bone pain when acetaminophen is already prescribed — June 2023 Q38. Counting IV and oral acetaminophen together after a rib fracture — June 2025 Q50.":["u92"],"copy|Anticipating nausea, persistent constipation and withdrawal when starting or stopping opioids — 2020 Q11 and June 2022 Q78. Recognizing opioid-induced hyperalgesia when pain increases as the dose rises — June 2025 Q14.":["u93"],"copy|Treating painful diabetic neuropathy at a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min — 2020 Q100. Choosing duloxetine for function-limiting back pain and depressed mood — September 2024 Q13.":["u94"],"copy|Matching neuropathic-pain drugs to sodium or blood pressure monitoring and recognizing anticholinergic toxicity — September 2024 Q17, June 2026 Q55, June 2025 Q4 and June 2025 Q79.":["u95"],"copy|Distinguishing elbow bursitis from arthritis on active and passive movement — May 2024 Q23. The exam cites Harrison 21e, ch. 370, p. 2844.":["u96"],"matching-pain-assessment-to-communication|Matching pain assessment to communication":["u98"],"classifying-the-pain-and-examining-a-painful-joint|Classifying the pain and examining a painful joint":["u99"],"choosing-analgesia-for-bone-pain-fracture-and-postoperative-recovery|Choosing analgesia for bone pain, fracture and postoperative recovery":["u100"],"opioid-counseling-tolerance-and-increasing-pain|Opioid counseling, tolerance and increasing pain":["u101"],"neuropathic-and-musculoskeletal-pain-which-drug-which-risk|Neuropathic and musculoskeletal pain: which drug, which risk?":["u102"],"goals-of-care-when-advanced-dementia-limits-eating|Goals of care when advanced dementia limits eating":["u103"],"israeli-context|Israeli context — אומדן כאב באשפוז גריאטרי ובקהילה":["u104"],"pain-over-time-activity-and-participation|Pain over time, activity and participation":["u105"],"when-a-procedure-can-improve-function|When a procedure can improve function":["u106"],"copy|Self-report remains the preferred way to measure pain.":["u107"],"copy|Ask “Does this hurt?”":["u108"],"copy|before assuming that a person with dementia cannot answer.":["u109"],"copy|Ask about the range over the last week, the location, onset, quality and triggers.":["u110"],"copy|A patient who reports little pain at rest may stop dressing, walking or sleeping because of pain with activity.":["u111"],"copy|Examine the painful area for inflammation, tenderness and restricted movement, and look for sensory loss, weakness or gait change.":["u112"],"copy|Review sleep, mood, recent falls, previous treatment, day-to-day function and what improvement would matter to the patient (p1058).":["u113"],"copy|A self-rating scale still requires a response.":["u114"],"copy|A vertical numeric scale may be easier to use than a horizontal one, but converting a sensation into a number can be difficult.":["u115"],"copy|The colored analog scale progresses from white to increasingly intense red and can work with or without cognitive impairment if the patient sees the colors, understands what they mean and can make a choice.":["u116"],"copy|A faces scale also requires visual acuity.":["u117"],"copy|A visual analog line is relatively abstract, with high failure rates and low reliability in older adults.":["u118"],"copy|The Iowa Pain Thermometer pairs verbal descriptors and numbers with a visual scale (p1059, Table 68-3).":["u119"],"copy|When self-report fails, observe at rest and during care.":["u120"],"copy|June 2023 Q23 describes agitation during care of a deep pressure injury in severe dementia.":["u121"],"copy|PAINAD uses breathing, vocalization, facial expression, body language and consolability.":["u122"],"copy|Compare the patient's behavior before and after an intervention.":["u123"],"copy|A caregiver can describe the person's baseline and a change during transfers.":["u124"],"copy|A number chosen by a relative cannot stand in for a patient's self-rating.":["u125"],"copy|Table 68-3 describes the self-report alternatives; Chapter 67 explicitly names PAINAD (p1048, 1058–1059).":["u126"],"copy|For chronic pain, track interference as well as intensity.":["u127"],"copy|The PEG asks about pain, enjoyment and general activity.":["u128"],"copy|The brief pain inventory includes location and functional interference.":["u129"],"copy|The geriatric pain measure asks about walking, social activity, tasks and sleep.":["u130"],"copy|WOMAC focuses on arthritis and the McGill questionnaire explores pain qualities, although both take more effort than a single rating (p1059, Table 68-4).":["u131"],"copy|Ischemic myocardium, inflamed skin and a mechanically stressed joint stimulate nociceptors.":["u132"],"copy|June 2026 Q26 therefore pairs angina with nociceptive pain.":["u133"],"copy|Neuropathic pain requires disease or a lesion of the somatosensory pathways.":["u134"],"copy|Diabetic neuropathy, postherpetic neuralgia and phantom limb pain offer different examples.":["u135"],"copy|Burning pain with sensory loss or pain from bedsheets touching the skin should prompt a focused neurologic examination.":["u136"],"copy|Mixed mechanisms can coexist and lead to different treatment choices (p1056–1058).":["u137"],"copy|Where the movement reproduces pain matters.":["u138"],"copy|With elbow bursitis, tenderness is usually focal over the periarticular structure.":["u139"],"copy|Active movement often hurts while passive movement is comparatively preserved.":["u140"],"copy|Intra-articular disease more often produces deep or diffuse pain with restriction on both active and passive movement.":["u141"],"copy|This is the distinction in May 2024 Q23.":["u142"],"copy|Its printed reference is Harrison 21e, ch. 370, p.":["u143"],"copy|2844, “Articular versus nonarticular.”":["u144"],"copy|Hazzard's examination paragraph covers range of motion and local tenderness more generally (p1058).":["u145"],"copy|After nonopioid treatment fails, discuss the expected functional benefit and choose a formulation that can be titrated to the pain pattern.":["u146"],"copy|For an opioid-naive older patient with severe metastatic bone pain already taking 3 g/day of acetaminophen, immediate-release single-agent oral oxycodone allows small dose adjustments without adding acetaminophen.":["u147"],"copy|Pregabalin targets a neuropathic component.":["u148"],"copy|Percocet adds an acetaminophen dose each time the oxycodone is increased.":["u149"],"copy|Continuous pain may require regular dosing, with reassessment as the illness or injury improves (p1060–1062, Table 68-6).":["u150"],"copy|A fentanyl patch is for established opioid tolerance.":["u151"],"copy|Verify the actual drug, dose and duration before conversion.":["u152"],"copy|The product label defines tolerance as at least one week at an adequate daily opioid dose, for example 60 mg oral morphine or 30 mg oral oxycodone daily, or an equianalgesic regimen.":["u153"],"copy|A few rescue doses, previous nonopioids, dysphagia or cancer pain alone do not meet that criterion.":["u154"],"copy|The patch has prolonged effects and can produce fatal respiratory depression when started in an opioid-naive patient (p1061, Table 68-6).":["u155"],"copy|Fentanyl transdermal prescribing information, sections 1 and 4.":["u156"],"copy|Choose the specific opioid and interval for prior exposure, frailty, renal function and the urgency of relief.":["u157"],"copy|The chapter suggests starting at about 25–50% of a younger adult's dose.":["u158"],"copy|Renal impairment can prolong exposure to the drug or its active metabolites, and sedating co-medication or COPD further narrows the margin of safety.":["u159"],"copy|Follow pain, movement, alertness, respiratory rate and bowel function together.":["u160"],"copy|Discuss safe administration with the patient or caregiver (p1059–1062).":["u161"],"copy|Count acetaminophen from every route and combination product.":["u162"],"copy|June 2025 Q50 gives 1 g IV four times daily.":["u163"],"copy|The patient has already received the book's 4 g/day maximum.":["u164"],"copy|The chapter lists 500–1000 mg every six hours and a lower 2–3 g/day maximum with hepatic impairment or a history of alcohol use.":["u165"],"copy|Supplemental oxygen may keep the pulse oximeter reading acceptable during hypoventilation.":["u166"],"copy|Consider continuous end-tidal CO₂ monitoring in high-risk perioperative patients, especially in the first 24 hours.":["u167"],"copy|COPD calls for caution and observation when severe rib pain needs relief (p1062).":["u168"],"copy|Enhanced recovery protocols use complementary mechanisms: acetaminophen, NSAIDs when appropriate and selected peripheral or neuraxial techniques can reduce opioid requirements.":["u169"],"copy|Poorly controlled postoperative pain delays mobility and can contribute to persistent pain.":["u170"],"copy|Nonselective NSAIDs can cause ulceration, bleeding, renal injury and cardiovascular events.":["u171"],"copy|Anticoagulation increases bleeding risk.":["u172"],"copy|COX-2 selectivity reduces some GI and platelet effects but retains renal and cardiovascular risk.":["u173"],"copy|Topical diclofenac on intact skin limits systemic exposure for localized OA.":["u174"],"copy|Table 68-7 specifically advises avoiding indomethacin and ketorolac and warns against prolonged systemic treatment with several other NSAIDs (p1061–1063).":["u175"],"copy|Nausea often improves after the first few days.":["u176"],"copy|Constipation commonly persists.":["u177"],"copy|Offer a regular bowel regimen when starting a strong opioid, review bowel frequency and reassess persistent nausea.":["u178"],"copy|Advise patients that initiation or a dose increase can impair concentration and driving.":["u179"],"copy|Reassess when stable.":["u180"],"copy|Physical dependence can produce withdrawal after abrupt cessation.":["u181"],"copy|A request for more medication warrants assessment of analgesia, function and misuse risk.":["u182"],"copy|Pure agonists have no universal analgesic ceiling, but escalating toxicity or diminishing benefit requires a change in plan.":["u183"],"copy|Combination products also carry their nonopioid ingredient's limit (p1060–1062).":["u184"],"copy|NICE CG140, managing side effects of strong opioids.":["u185"],"copy|With tolerance, the same dose gives less relief.":["u186"],"copy|With opioid-induced hyperalgesia, pain may worsen when the opioid is increased or spread beyond the original painful region, even without disease progression.":["u187"],"copy|Chronic exposure, particularly at high doses of potent opioids, increases suspicion.":["u188"],"copy|Reassess the diagnosis, consider a gradual reduction and follow the response.":["u189"],"copy|Distinguish diffuse allodynia during escalation from aches that begin after abrupt withdrawal (p1061).":["u190"],"copy|Page 1060 summarizes the US 2016 opioid guideline, including a review within one to four weeks and at least every three months thereafter.":["u191"],"copy|CDC 2022 advises reassessing before reaching 50 morphine milligram equivalents per day, adding follow-up and offering naloxone when indicated.":["u192"],"copy|Dosage is a guidepost for an individual review.":["u193"],"copy|There is no fixed 90-MME ceiling.":["u194"],"copy|The 2022 outpatient recommendations exclude cancer-related pain treatment, palliative care, end-of-life care and pain related to sickle cell disease.":["u195"],"copy|CDC 2022 guideline, recommendations 4, 7 and 8.":["u196"],"copy|Duloxetine enhances descending pain inhibition through serotonin and norepinephrine.":["u197"],"copy|It can address musculoskeletal or neuropathic pain and coexisting depression.":["u198"],"copy|September 2024 Q13 describes persistent low-back pain limiting activity after physical therapy and simple analgesia.":["u199"],"copy|Duloxetine 30 mg/day is a reasonable starting choice among the options.":["u200"],"copy|There is no opioid tolerance for a fentanyl patch or clear neuropathic indication for starting pregabalin at 300 mg/day.":["u201"],"copy|For June 2026 Q55, measure blood pressure before starting duloxetine and periodically during treatment.":["u203"],"copy|The listed Hazzard pages describe analgesic classes and their adverse effects; the prescribing information specifies the blood pressure monitoring.":["u204"],"copy|SNRIs are less 